WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.890
Well, we've seen another really resounding
defeat for the anti-abortion crowd.

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:13.290
The Supreme Court yesterday said that, no,
you can't require in Louisiana on doctors

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:22.960
who perform abortions to have admitting privileges
at area hospitals, that this effectively violates

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.500
Roe v. Wade, which is the precedent of note
and thus reminds us why this question of Roe

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:35.250
v. Wade comes up so often when new potential
Supreme Court justices are being vetted and

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:36.320
questioned.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:43.090
And what is particularly interesting about
this decision is that it was a five four decision

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:51.120
where the five the majority was made up of
the four more liberal justices and Chief Justice

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.460
John Roberts, a moderate conservative.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.041
Certainly no doubt about it.

00:00:57.041 --> 00:01:00.930
And we're going to talk more about Chief Justice
John Roberts and his legacy and his sort of

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.320
place within the Supreme Court on Thursday.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.890
But for today, I want to talk about this decision
that this really is an important defeat for

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:15.840
the anti choice movement that seeks to just
ban abortion, make abortion illegal, despite

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:23.440
the reality, of course, that banning abortion
reduces legal abortion, maybe at least in

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.330
the state where it's banned.

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:31.760
But it does not actually reduce the number
of women who figure out a way to terminate

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:32.760
a pregnancy.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.270
And that's another story that we've talked
about quite a bit on this program.

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:40.750
So the reason this is an important decision
is that this is a common way to try to restrict

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:45.920
access to abortion the way it works in this
Louisiana law that was struck down by the

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.790
Supreme Court yesterday.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:52.290
And in other states, the game that they play
essentially is you.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:59.360
You don't ban abortion, per say, but you require
doctors who perform abortions to have admitting

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.990
privileges at nearby hospitals.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:10.029
And the anti-abortion people say that this
is that the reason you do this is, well, if

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:17.529
a doctor performs an abortion and the patient
needs to be hospitalized, it's important that

00:02:17.529 --> 00:02:24.480
the doctor have admitting privileges so that
that doctor can continue providing care in

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:28.659
the hospital for the hospitalized abortion
patient.

00:02:28.659 --> 00:02:35.950
Now, all of this is part of the ruse that
includes making abortion seem like a far more

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.639
risky medical procedure than it actually is.

00:02:38.639 --> 00:02:41.349
So a few realities that we should keep in
mind.

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:46.379
Number one, hospitalizations after abortion
are very, very rare.

00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:48.200
Number two.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:54.019
Even if one were necessary a hospitalization,
the patient would still be able to get care,

00:02:54.019 --> 00:02:58.069
even if the doctor that performed the abortion
doesn't have admitted admitting privileges

00:02:58.069 --> 00:02:59.239
at the local hospital.

00:02:59.239 --> 00:03:05.669
And number three, there are bureaucratic reasons
that sometimes make it difficult for doctors

00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:08.799
who perform abortions to get those admitting
privileges.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:13.359
So this is essentially a trick from the anti
choice crowd to make abortions more difficult

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:17.790
to obtain and maybe to dissuade people from
seeking them out in general.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.190
So so understand how crazy this is.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:26.010
Five doctors in all of Louisiana provide abortions.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.099
Two of them have obtained admitting privileges.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:31.650
One in New Orleans and one in Shreveport.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:36.760
The Shreveport doctor says he can't handle
all of the work alone.

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:45.699
And the trial judge concluded that what this
law, in effect, does is reduce Louisiana to

00:03:45.699 --> 00:03:50.629
a single doctor in a single clinic in New
Orleans.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:54.859
And that is in practice a violation of Roe
v. Wade.

00:03:54.859 --> 00:03:59.920
That is ultimately what the Supreme Court
found, that the Roe v. Wade decision from

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:05.900
1973, which is the precedent for so much of
the discussion about abortion today, would

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:13.249
in practice be violated if this law requiring
admitting privileges at local hospitals were

00:04:13.249 --> 00:04:14.650
to remain the law of the land.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:17.209
So this is relevant for a few different reasons.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:22.740
Number one, Chief Justice John Roberts, a
George W. Bush appointee, previously favored

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.620
abortion restrictions in two other abortion
related cases.

00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.819
We're going to talk more about him momentarily.

00:04:28.819 --> 00:04:35.539
But in this case, John Roberts coming over
with the majority in defense of abortion rights.

00:04:35.539 --> 00:04:41.449
Number two, this is huge because everything
is just going wrong for this administration.

00:04:41.449 --> 00:04:44.710
Donald Trump, as you may know, now claims
to be against abortion.

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:49.710
He was in favor of a woman's right to choose,
I believe his for 67 or 68 years of life.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.120
His evangelical followers have fallen for
it hook, line and sinker.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.300
I guess they believe Trump is now against
abortion.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:00.860
And this is another decision that goes against
Donald Trump stated wishes.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:03.939
If we believe that those are actually his
which wishes.

00:05:03.939 --> 00:05:09.490
Now, let's talk about Chief Justice John Roberts,
because this is really, for me, the crux of

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:11.669
the discussion that now is going on on Friday.

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:13.949
I'm sorry, on Thursday.

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:20.400
I'm going to get into more detail about whether
Chief Justice John Roberts is a moderate or

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:21.400
a conservative.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:24.220
Of course, he was selected by George W. Bush.

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:29.349
But what I really want you to understand about
this decision and the decision on Dacca last

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:35.220
week where John Roberts was also on the side
of we're not going to remove Dacca for the

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.900
reason the Trump administration wants it removed.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.139
These are technical decisions.

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:46.600
These are not decisions where John Roberts
is coming over to the left, so to speak, on

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:51.050
the merits of abortion or on the merits of
DACA.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:55.520
What I mean by that is in the dock, a case,
for example, the Trump administration just

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:56.710
bungled their case.

00:05:56.710 --> 00:06:03.009
The Trump administration tried to end DACA
by saying Barack Obama didn't have the authority

00:06:03.009 --> 00:06:07.090
to put DACA in place via executive order.

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.870
The Supreme Court said that's not a good argument.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:16.960
The Supreme Court, John Roberts, they correctly
found that Obama did not overstep authority,

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.789
but it was not a decision on the merits of
DACA.

00:06:19.789 --> 00:06:26.349
And similarly with this abortion decision,
John Roberts is saying, in a technical sense,

00:06:26.349 --> 00:06:30.419
the Louisiana law would violate the precedent
set by Roe v. Wade.

00:06:30.419 --> 00:06:31.930
It's a technical decision.

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:33.250
It's not a decision on the merits.

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.590
Now, will we take it?

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.060
Of course we will.

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:41.750
But what's important to understand, I mean,
look at even the LGBT discrimination case.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:48.840
Roberts correctly identified in a technical
sense, if you allow.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.620
An employer to discriminate on the basis of
sexual orientation or gender identity.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:07:00.129
By definition, it is a discriminatory act
on the basis of sex which is banned.

00:07:00.129 --> 00:07:02.020
So what John Roberts did.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:05.009
It doesn't mean he made the wrong decisions.

00:07:05.009 --> 00:07:09.889
It means he is simply getting the technical
question right.

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:16.860
It shows us actually how insane the court
is that these decisions were not nine to zero.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.969
This decision on abortion should have been
nine to zero.

00:07:19.969 --> 00:07:27.169
The law that Louisiana wanted to pass on abortion
obviously violates Roe v. Wade.

00:07:27.169 --> 00:07:29.449
It should have been a nine zero decision.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:33.639
So the message here should not be who cares
about judges?

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:35.610
We got the right decision anyway.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:40.509
Even with Chief Justice John Roberts, who
came over and joined the liberal majority,

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:46.910
the decision should have been nine to zero
and it shouldn't come down to one center.

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:48.599
Right justice deciding.

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:50.810
Oh, precedent matters.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:51.810
Precedent matters.

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:56.280
That's why we need Joe Biden and not Donald
Trump choosing more justices, because this

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.310
should have been should have been a nine zero
decision.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:04.070
And then on Thursday, we're going to talk
a little bit more, sort of about the political

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:07.860
orientation of Chief Justice John Roberts
and other justices on the court.

